        {
          "@id": "urn:uuid:4e5e2f5a-d721-5784-8baa-9fe1149c929d",
          "@type": "uco-observable:ExtractedStringsFacet",
          "uco-observable:strings": [
            {
              "@id": "urn:uuid:33218600-27b4-50ea-aaf0-c92cc792dc11",
              "@type": "uco-observable:ExtractedString",
              "uco-observable:stringValue": "Case 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 48 Entered on FLSD Docket 08/15/2024 Page 1 of 5\n\nUNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT\nSOUTHERN DISTRICT OF FLORIDA\n\nCASE NO. 23-80053-1-CR-CANNON\n\nUNITED STATES OF AMERICA\n\nvs.\n\nKEITH DUANE MCINTOSH,\n\nDefendant.\n- - - - - - - -- - - -- - - -I\nFACTUAL PROFFER\n\nThe United States of America and Keith Duane McIntosh (\"Defendant\" or \"MCINTOSH\")\n\nagree that if this case had gone to trial, the United States would have proven the following facts\n\nbeyond a reasonable doubt:\n\n1. At all relevant times, \"Website A\" was a website dedicated to the advertisement\n\nand distribution of child pornography, as defined in 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2256(8)(A), and the discussion of\n\nchild sexual abuse. Users of Website A could engage in online communications with other users.\n\nUsers trafficked in child pornography via Website A by posting web links within messages.\n\nThese links redirected to other websites, such as file-hosting websites, where the linked-to child\n\npornography images and videos were available for download and/or viewing. Under the rules of\n\nWebsite A, the links were required to contain descriptive text that provided information about the\n\nsexual acts and age and gender of the child victim depicted in the linked-to child pornography\n\nfiles. During the timeframe relevant to this matter, Website A users made tens or hundreds of\n\nthousands of child pornography images and videos available in this manner. At all relevant times,\n\nWebsite A was managed by particular users referred to as \"staff' and it had at least 100 registered\n\nmembers. Website A stopped functioning in its original form in approximately November 2022.\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 48 Entered on FLSD Docket 08/15/2024 Page 2 of 5\n\n2. MCINTOSH had been a registered member of Website A since early 2018. For\n\nyears, he was the approximately fourth highest-ranking administrator of Website A. He, along with\n\napproximately five other high-ranking administrators of the website, had access to an exclusive\n\nroom on Website A where he and the others voted on decisions regarding the operation of the\n\nwebsite, such as who should be kicked out and who should get promoted. In one particular\n\ninstance, he advised other website administrators that a user needed to post \"pthc\" content on\n\nWebsite A in order to receive a promotion. \"pthc\" stands for \"pre-teen hard core\" and generally\n\nrefers to depictions of prepubescent children being sexually penetrated. Following this\n\nconversation, the other user posted such content on Website A. Additionally, in his role as a high-\n\nranking administrator, he had the power to recommend promotion of users to the head\n\nadministrator of the website and was one of five individuals who had the power to vote on user\n\npromotions, he monitored Website A' s email accounts for donations from other users and\n\nforwarded these donations to the head administrator of the website, he developed Website A' s\n\n\"Frequently Asked Questions\" section, and he backed up data from different rooms on Website A\n\nonto his local storage drives.\n\n3. As of June 28, 2022, MCINTOSH had posted several thousand messages on\n\nWebsite A. On one occasion, he advised users of Website A in a private room that another member\n\nhad contacted law enforcement and wrote that \"[t]his should also serve as a reminder to always\n\ntreat everybody as though they could be [a law enforcement agent] or could be providing\n\ninformation to [a law enforcement agent]. Never share personal information with anybody!! \" In\n\nanother post, he advised staff members of Website A of the following: \"We are creating [a Website\n\nA] FAQ. Please mail me any questions (and, hopefully , the associated answers !) you feel should\n\nbe included. Thanks! \"\n\n2\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 48 Entered on FLSD Docket 08/15/2024 Page 3 of 5\n\n4. Until approximately July 2022, and during the time when MCINTOSH engaged in\n\nthe conduct on Website A described above, another high-ranking administrator of Website A\n\nresided in Palm Beach County, Florida, within the Southern District of Florida, where he\n\nadvertised and distributed child pornography and took acts to help manage Website A from his\n\nhome.\n\n5. Additionally, after Website A ceased operating in approximately November 2022,\n\nMCINTOSH moved to a different website on the dark web dedicated to the advertisement and\n\ndistribution of child pornography and the discussion of child sexual abuse, where he continued to\n\nactively participate and post comments. Following his arrest, MCINTOSH told law enforcement\n\nthat he occasionally posted child pornography on Website A but did not do so frequently because\n\nhe did not have to due to his high-ranking status on the website.\n\n6. Further, beginning on an unknown date and continuing until the date of his arrest\n\non or about March 28, 2024, MCINTOSH possessed multiple electronic devices in his residence\n\nin the Western District of Michigan that he used to engage in the above and related conduct and\n\nthat contained child pornography, links to websites on the dark web dedicated to the advertisement\n\nand distribution of child pornography and the discussion of child sexual abuse, and/or documents\n\ndescribing the sexual abuse of children. These devices contained evidence that MCINTOSH used\n\nencryption and similar programs in an attempt to conceal this activity. MCINTOSH agrees that\n\nthe following devices are subject to forfeiture:\n\na. Three SanDisk flash drives; and\n\nb. A Seagate 2 TB external hard drive.\n\n7. MCINTOSH has a prior conviction for receipt of child pornography, in violation\n\nof Chapter 110 of Title 18, United States Code, Section 2252(a)(2), in the United States District\n\n3\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 48 Entered on FLSD Docket 08/15/2024 Page 4 of 5\n\nCourt for the District of New Jersey, case number 94-cr-652.\n\n8. Based on these facts and other information known to the parties, MCINTOSH\n\nagrees that, had this case gone to trial, the government would have proven beyond a reasonable\n\ndoubt, as to Count 1 of the Superseding Indictment, that MCINTOSH conspired to advertise child\n\npornography in violation of Title 18, United States Code, Section 2251(d) and (e), beginning in at\n\nleast March 2018 and continuing through on or about November 14, 2022, such that:\n\na. two or more persons in some way agreed to try to accomplish a shared and unlawful\nplan;\n\nb. the Defendant knew the unlawful purpose of the plan and willfully joined in it.\n\nAnd that the unlawful purpose of the plan was to commit the offense of advertising child\n\npornography in violation of Title 18, United States Code, Section 2251(d) and (e), such that:\n\na. the Defendant knowingly made, printed, or published, or caused to be made,\nprinted, and published, a notice or advertisement seeking or offering;\n\nb. to produce, display, distribute, or reproduce any visual depiction involving the use\nof a minor engaged in sexually explicit conduct, and the visual depiction is of such\nconduct; and\n\nc. the Defendant knows or has reason to know that the notice or advertisement would\nbe transported using any means or facility of interstate or foreign commerce or in\nor affecting interstate or foreign commerce by any means, including by computer\nor mailed; or\n\nd. such notice or advertisement was transported using any means or facility of\ninterstate or foreign commerce or in or affecting interstate or foreign commerce by\nany means, including by computer or mailed.\n\n9. Based on these facts and other information known to the parties, MCINTOSH also\n\nagrees that, had this case gone to trial, the government would have proven beyond a reasonable\n\ndoubt, as to Count 2 of the Superseding Indictment, that MCINTOSH conspired to distribute child\n\npornography in violation of Title 18, United States Code, Section 2252A( a)(2) and (b )(1 ),\n\nbeginning in at least March 2018 and continuing through on or about November 14, 2022, such\n\n4\nCase 9:24-cr-80053-AMC Document 48 Entered on FLSD Docket 08/15/2024 Page 5 of 5\n\nthat:\n\na. two or more persons in some way agreed to try to accomplish a shared and unlawful\nplan;\n\nb. the Defendant knew the unlawful purpose of the plan and willfully joined in it.\n\nAnd that the unlawful purpose of the plan was to commit the offense of distributing child\n\npornography in violation of Title 18, United States Code, Section 2252A( a)(2) and (b )(1 ), such\n\nthat:\n\na. the Defendant knowingly distributed an item or items of child pornography;\n\nb. the item or items of child pornography had been distributed using any means or\nfacility of interstate or foreign commerce or had been mailed, or shipped or\ntransported in or affecting interstate or foreign commerce by any means, including\nby computer; and\n\nc. when the Defendant distributed the items, the Defendant believed the items were\nor contained child pornography.\n\nMARKENZY LAPOINTE\nUNITED STATES ATTORNEY\n\nDate: By:\n\nDate: By:\n\nWILLIAM CLAYMAN\nTRIAL ATTORNEYS, U.S. DEP 'T OF JUSTICE\n\nDate: '6/4~2Jf\" By:\n~-a.~\nRICHARD A. SERAFINI\nATTORNEY FOR DEFENDANT /\n,\n\nDate ~/w.t By: t#!t~IJ4ll 71/t J'/;,/\nKEITH DUANE MCINTOSH\nDEFENDANT\n\n5"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
